    {
      "id": "6367de60-176c-4fe5-9f9b-cc743fb4a16b",
      "name": "Why MCP is awesome",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is awesome",
      "docTypeId": "8e5380de-a960-4f13-8d09-2a3e297294d9",
      "templateId": "984fa8d0-f04b-48eb-96ff-c295179c6441",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP is awesome"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "summary",
          "value": "The Model Context Protocol gives AI assistants one standard, auditable way to reach the systems we already run."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-07 00:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "heroImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "c1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
              "mediaKey": "ed0b5f1f-7d5a-46c1-b8bb-c6dd5022ab65",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": null,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": null,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": null,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "e8431c7e-8dc2-456d-b12d-e27e2ee8a264",
                "key": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Every integration used to be a bespoke adapter. Each assistant, each tool and each CMS needed its own glue code, its own auth story and its own idea of what a request looks like.</p><p>The Model Context Protocol (MCP) replaces that patchwork with one contract. A server exposes capabilities as named tools with typed inputs. A client discovers them and calls them. That is the whole idea, and it is a good one.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "fdffd23b-48fa-4595-9189-cbbcc1399648",
                "key": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "d1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                        "mediaKey": "53080f70-5b48-4a0e-ad6d-62b06742e5a2",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "Photo by Alexandre Debiève on Unsplash"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "e8431c7e-8dc2-456d-b12d-e27e2ee8a264",
                "key": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Why it is awesome: <strong>it is boring in the right ways.</strong> The tools are declared with schemas, so mistakes are caught at the boundary. The same server works for any MCP client, and because the interface is explicit you can read exactly what an agent is allowed to do.</p><p>In this project, the Umbraco MCP server lets an agent model document types, create content and manage media without a single click in the back office.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "a1000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/why-mcp-is-awesome/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    },
    {
      "id": "da38daa0-caf5-4748-b6b3-91c5ba9d349e",
      "name": "Why MCP is awesome, part 2",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is awesome, part 2",
      "docTypeId": "8e5380de-a960-4f13-8d09-2a3e297294d9",
      "templateId": "984fa8d0-f04b-48eb-96ff-c295179c6441",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP is awesome, part 2"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "summary",
          "value": "Content without the clicking: how an MCP server turns a CMS into something an agent can build with."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-07 00:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "heroImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "c2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
              "mediaKey": "cf4a4a9f-a45d-46ee-98fc-bf6994cd6a96",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "body",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": null,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": null,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": null,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "e8431c7e-8dc2-456d-b12d-e27e2ee8a264",
                "key": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>In part one we covered the idea. Here is what it feels like in practice.</p><p>Modelling content is a sequence of small, checkable steps: create an element type, move it into the right folder, create the data type it needs, then read it back to confirm it took. Each step is a tool call with a clear result.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "fdffd23b-48fa-4595-9189-cbbcc1399648",
                "key": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "d2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                        "mediaKey": "7f05315a-e063-4335-b1c6-3d73db121e73",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "caption",
                    "value": "Photo by Vardan Papikyan on Unsplash"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "e8431c7e-8dc2-456d-b12d-e27e2ee8a264",
                "key": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "body",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>The part I like most is the feedback loop. When a block payload is wrong it fails quietly, so the habit is to read structural writes back straight away. MCP makes that easy because reading is just another tool, and the agent and the developer end up looking at the same facts.</p><p>MCP is awesome because it makes the boring plumbing legible. That leaves more room for the interesting decisions.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "b2000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/why-mcp-is-awesome-part-2/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    }
